  "content": {
    "caseIdentity": "K.T. Plantation Pvt. Ltd. v. State of Karnataka, (2011) 9 SCC 1",
    "court": "Supreme Court of India",
    "decisionYear": 2011,
    "citation": "(2011) 9 SCC 1",
    "facts": "The Court considered compulsory acquisition and constitutional property protection.",
    "questionsBeforeCourt": [
      "What does Article 300A require when property is deprived by authority of law?"
    ],
    "ratioDecidendi": "Deprivation must have authority of law and cannot be arbitrary; compensation principles remain relevant to constitutional review even though property is no longer a Fundamental Right.",
    "holding": "Right to property under Article 300A is a constitutional and human right; \"authority of law\" inherently requires a valid statute, public purpose, and fair/non-illusory compensation under Article 14.",
    "relevance": "Locus classicus on the modern interpretation of Article 300A.",
